From 5632c848ee7c1563092dcb5f971b66ced67a10dc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 18:18:02 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] microblaze: use bsefi and bsifi for bit-fields

MicroBlaze v10.0 added two barrel-shifter instructions with an
immediate width and shift: bsefi extracts a field, zero-extended, and
bsifi inserts the low bits of one register into a field of another.
gcc has no pattern for either, so a bit-field read is a shift and a
mask, and a write is a mask, a shift, a second mask and an add.

Add extzvsi and insvsi for -mcpu=v10.0 and later with
-mxl-barrel-shift.  They accept a field of 1 to 31 bits that fits in
the word; the assembler rejects a 32-bit bsefi, and a 32-bit field is a
move.  There is no extv, because bsefi cannot sign-extend, so a signed
field still uses a shift pair.  The expander and insn layout follows
extzv<mode> and insv<mode> in mips.md, whose ext and ins take the same
position and size immediates.

For "struct s { unsigned a : 3; unsigned b : 7; unsigned c : 22; }",
reading p->b goes from bsrli and andi to one bsefi, and writing it goes
from andi, bslli, andi and addk to one bsifi.

QEMU implements both instructions.  The execution test checks unsigned
and signed fields and that the neighbouring fields are preserved.

Assisted-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

